# Process

Running another program and reading what it answered — the substrate a LOGOS program drives a
compiler, a proof checker, an exporter or any other command-line tool with.

A run is always BOUNDED. It names how long it may take and how much output it may keep, and both
bounds are checked before anything starts: a tool that hangs is stopped at its deadline, and a tool
that floods its output keeps running to completion while only the first `maxOutput` bytes of each
stream are kept. Nothing a run starts outlives it — the program runs as the leader of a process group
of its own, and when the run ends, by finishing or by its deadline, every process left in that group
is stopped too.

There is no shell. `program` is the executable (a path, or a name searched on `PATH`) and `args` are
handed to it exactly as written: a space, a `$`, a `*` or a quote inside an argument is that
character, never syntax. A program that wants a pipeline runs a shell itself, by name, and says so.

The child's standard input is empty. Its environment is the running program's own, with each entry
of `env` laid over it.

Inside a `## Test` the world is sealed, and a sealed world starts no processes: a call answers `Err`
unless the test grants `This test may use the real processes.` and runs under `largo test --live`.

## Note
What one finished run answers.

`exitCode` is the code the program exited with. A program stopped by a signal has no code of its
own, so it answers 128 plus the signal's number, the convention every POSIX shell reports (a run
stopped at its deadline is killed by signal 9 and answers 137). `timedOut` says whether the deadline
stopped it. `stdout` and `stderr` are what it wrote, decoded as UTF-8 with any invalid byte replaced
by U+FFFD; `truncated` says whether either stream was longer than `maxOutput` bytes and was cut there.

## A ProcessResult has:
    an exitCode, which is Int.
    a stdout, which is Text.
    a stderr, which is Text.
    a timedOut, which is Bool.
    a truncated, which is Bool.

## Note
The engine's half of `runProcess`: the same run, answering its five parts as Text. Call `runProcess`.

The parts are, in order, the exit code, standard output, standard error, then `true` or `false` for
timed-out and truncated — so that no engine has to build a record of its own. `env` is a list of
`NAME=value` entries; `runProcess` takes the environment as a map and answers the record.

## To native runProcessParts (program: Text) and (args: Seq of Text) and (cwd: Text) and (env: Seq of Text) and (timeoutMs: Int) and (maxOutput: Int) -> Result of Seq of Text and Text

## Note
Runs `program` with `args`, bounded by `timeoutMs` and `maxOutput`, and answers what it did.

`cwd` is the directory it runs in (the empty Text keeps the running program's own), `env` is laid
over the inherited environment, the run lasts at most `timeoutMs` milliseconds, and at most
`maxOutput` bytes of each output stream are kept.

`Err` means the run never happened: the program could not be started, the directory does not exist,
an environment name is empty or holds `=`, a bound is out of range, or the world is sealed. A
program that started and then failed — a non-zero exit, a deadline — is an `Ok` whose record says so,
because that failure is the program's answer, not the caller's mistake.

## To runProcess (program: Text) and (args: Seq of Text) and (cwd: Text) and (env: Map of Text to Text) and (timeoutMs: Int) and (maxOutput: Int) -> Result of ProcessResult and Text:
    Let mutable pairs be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for (name, value) in env:
        Push "{name}={value}" to pairs.
    Inspect runProcessParts(program, args, cwd, pairs, timeoutMs, maxOutput):
        When Ok (parts):
            Let code be parseInt(item 1 of parts).
            Let out be item 2 of parts.
            Let err be item 3 of parts.
            Let late be item 4 of parts is equal to "true".
            Let cut be item 5 of parts is equal to "true".
            Return a new Ok with value (a new ProcessResult with exitCode code and stdout out and stderr err and timedOut late and truncated cut).
        When Err (why):
            Return a new Err with error why.
